WEBVTT

0
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.760
The analysis phase of the ADI model has 4 main components

1
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:12.210
needs analysis,

2
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.609
learner analysis,

3
00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:14.930
task analysis,

4
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.920
and context analysis.

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:17.670
Together,

6
00:00:17.729 --> 00:00:20.059
these components provide the information needed

7
00:00:20.059 --> 00:00:22.500
to make effective instructional decisions.

8
00:00:22.979 --> 00:00:23.860
Number 1,

9
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.559
needs analysis.

10
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.100
Needs analysis helps determine whether training

11
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.600
or instruction is actually necessary.

12
00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:34.650
It focuses on identifying the performance gap,

13
00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:38.759
which is the difference between current performance and desired performance.

14
00:00:39.069 --> 00:00:41.220
It also looks for the cause of the problem

15
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.799
because not every issue can be solved through instruction.

16
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.009
2,

17
00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:47.529
learner analysis.

18
00:00:48.599 --> 00:00:51.990
Learner analysis focuses on understanding the audience.

19
00:00:52.279 --> 00:00:55.819
Instructional designers examine learner characteristics such as

20
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:56.950
prior knowledge,

21
00:00:57.119 --> 00:00:57.479
skills,

22
00:00:57.549 --> 00:00:58.279
motivation,

23
00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.340
demographics,

24
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:00.950
and learning preferences.

25
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:03.560
This helps ensure that instruction is relevant,

26
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:04.309
appropriate,

27
00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:05.879
and engaging for the learners.

28
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.569
3

29
00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.050
task analysis.

30
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.260
Task analysis breaks a complex task into smaller parts.

31
00:01:14.559 --> 00:01:15.949
It identifies the knowledge,

32
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:16.360
skills,

33
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.750
and procedures learners need in order to perform successfully.

34
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:23.000
This helps instructional designers decide exactly what

35
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.919
should be taught and in what sequence.

36
00:01:26.129 --> 00:01:27.199
Number 4,

37
00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:28.879
context analysis.

38
00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:34.110
Context analysis examines the setting where learning takes place

39
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.239
and the setting where learners will apply what they have learned.

40
00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.930
This includes the tools,

41
00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:39.730
resources,

42
00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:40.519
limitations,

43
00:01:40.529 --> 00:01:42.529
and conditions of both environments.

44
00:01:43.239 --> 00:01:47.510
Understanding the context helps make instruction more practical and useful.